{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat R9B-BRIEF.md collab/yang-clojure/1794700000000-engineer-d1-r9b-corpus-rebuild.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794698000000-engineer-d1-r9-jupiter.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-11-14 04:2x GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-11-13 23:2x EST\nCoding-Agent: codex\nSession-ID: (resume of thread 01a1d916-5204-7cc0-8c44-b6a552dd37d7 — jupiter)\n\n# Task: D1 r9 continuation — rebuild the corpus with persisted inputs on jupiter, then the bounds + tagged run\n\nRole: VM Engineer (compiler provenance)\n\nImplementers:\n- Model: gpt-6.1-terra | Assigned: 2026-11-14 04:2x UTC | Status: active | Rationale: continuity on the jupiter thread; the owner directs POOL 2 be drawn down\n\n## State resume\n\nYour r9 returned incomplete with the exact reasons: the r8 measurement store was deleted (its outcomes file retained outcomes, not input/snapshot addresses), and jupiter's heap/trace bounds are unmeasured. Your static pilot passed (1/4), the standing battery passed (57/700/0), the v1 table hash unchanged, nothing committed.\n\nThe laptop corpus run's findings chain (r1-r8 + the two astra rulings + the rebind note) is in the worktree's `collab/yang-clojure/` — read the r8 findings (`1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.glm-5.3.findings.md`) for the corpus-run pattern you are replicating: fresh recorded admissions (scope + ordinal per fixture), the full static gate per fixture, per-fixture outcomes with input/snapshot addresses RETAINED THIS TIME, batched with the outcomes file as the progress record.\n\n## The work (in order; this supersedes your r9 brief's sequence)\n\n1. **The corpus rebuild on jupiter**: all 155 fixtures through the full static gate under fresh recorded admissions (the r8 pattern), WITH the retained input/snapshot addresses persisted per-fixture this time (that was r8's gap — the outcomes file must carry what reconstruction needs). Batched; the outcomes file is the progress record; a killed batch loses nothing.\n2. **The jupiter bounds measurement**: from the rebuilt corpus's actual data — peak heap (named method + comparability limits), lane quantities, serialized sizes, per-phase elapsed — declared as finite ceilings with headroom, max-based pass/fail (the cc3 v2 format; the draft `1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md` supplies the shape).\n3. **The tagged verification run UNDER the jupiter declaration**: the independent verifier executes the scoped attribution query over both C lanes with the ceilings enforced. A ceiling exceedance is a failed bounded run, recorded honestly — never a raised ceiling.\n4. **The verification-result publication** + the companion record: `slice9_verification.edn` (or sibling) + `slice9_correspondence.edn` per the original brief — with the retention-location note (the OWNER DECISION is pending; construct and store in the worktree's scratch store; do NOT commit the large store).\n\nIf the turn cannot complete all four: deliver the next coherent partial with the same honesty standard (the corpus rebuild with retained addresses is the priority — it unblocks everything else).\n\n## Bounds/invariants/deliverable\n\nSame as r9's brief: bounded files; forbidden set unchanged; the v1 bytes frozen (SHA e435ac40...); origins only from retained coordinates; the verifier rejects, never substitutes; red-first per item; kondo 0 errors. Deliverable: findings (worktree mirror; `1794700000000-engineer-d1-r9b-corpus-rebuild.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md` or your naming), updating the r9 findings by reference. Commit NOTHING.\n\nBegin the final response exactly with:\nCompleted-GMT: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS GMT>\nCompleted-Local: <YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS local-timezone-name>\n# D1 r9b Jupiter: corpus rebuild started, incomplete\n\nThis updates `1794698000000-engineer-d1-r9-jupiter.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md` by reference. **Partial only: 8 of 155 fixtures passed the static gate; 147 remain not run.** No Jupiter bounds declaration, tagged run, D1 corpus record, verification result, or companion has been completed. No commit; the retention-location owner decision remains pending.\n\nThe batch pass outcome now retains the six actual segment addresses returned by persistence: input, snapshot, evidence, profile, root and stamped-root. The batch record refuses a pass missing any of these addresses. A storage delta of zero is permitted because deduplicated replay can add no bytes. The record remains bookkeeping, not a verification result. Each admission is generated by the existing fresh run and recorded with its origin; no historical coordinates are invented.\n\nScratch progress is `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 at this stopping point `cfffefb46cebc49c74a44b6db739d32bb5e8bd631d3b382d9d9b6faf773f1f3b`), with its store at `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` (26,292,420 bytes). These are local scratch, not published retention. The first process was interrupted after seven recorded passes; a fresh process reopened the store and progress file and added one pass. A separate fresh process then reopened the store, fetched all 48 addresses for the eight passes, and checked that each input and snapshot origin equalled the recorded admission origin. This demonstrates process-interruption resume for this sample, not a power-loss test or the completed corpus.\n\nRed first: an outcome missing addresses was accepted before the change and refused afterward; a zero-byte pass was refused before the change and accepted afterward. The focused batch test passes 1 test / 9 assertions. Before the zero-byte adjustment the standing battery passed 57 tests / 702 assertions; the focused test covers the subsequent adjustment. The restarted corpus invocation passed 1 test / 9 assertions. clj-kondo reports 0 errors and 0 warnings on the three changed files. The frozen `slice9_site_tables.edn` SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. No full oracle suite or tagged lane was run.\n\nTo resume, keep both scratch paths together, set `target/d1-corpus-run.edn` to their paths and the desired `:D1_CORPUS_BATCH`, then run `clojure -M:test -n yang.latte-oracle-test -v yang.latte-oracle-test/test-d1-corpus-static-gates` with the configured JVM environment. Outcomes are final, and the progress file identifies the remaining fixtures. After the full rebuild, measure actual Jupiter lane, heap, size and phase maxima before declaring ceilings; do not borrow laptop bounds. The r9 limitations on verifier completeness and provenance remain unresolved and must be resolved before any verification-result claim.\n\n## Continuation on 2026-11-16\n\nThe existing run resumed with a 25-fixture batch, using the same outcomes file and store, with spot checks enabled. The invocation completed successfully (1 test, 9 assertions, 0 failures/errors); all 25 new outcomes passed. A separate process reopened the store and fetched all six retained addresses for each of the 33 passes, and checked that each fetched input and snapshot origin matched its recorded admission origin. Its summary was **155 fixtures: 33 pass, 0 refused, 122 not run; 198 addresses checked**. This is still a partial static corpus rebuild, not a verification result.\n\nAt this stopping point `target/d1-r9b-outcomes.edn` SHA-256 is `ea2b6829d6a33a5bfe32e312dc6b93e91e4181e633b9c46de45471e2227264a8`; `target/d1-r9b-store/content.jing` is 154,053,237 bytes. The batch log is `target/d1-r9b-batch-2.log` and the reopen check is `target/d1-r9b-check-2.log`. The check was run using `java -cp \"$(clojure -Spath -A:test)\" clojure.main target/d1-r9b-check.clj`; two preliminary attempts accidentally invoked the test alias's broad default suite and were interrupted, so they are not completed test results. The frozen table SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`.\n\nNo code was changed in this continuation. No Jupiter bounds declaration, tagged lane, corpus correspondence record, verification result, or companion has been completed. No commit was made. Preserve both scratch paths together; the configured next batch remains 25 fixtures. The previous red-first and kondo results above describe the preexisting changes, not new tests from this continuation. The r9 verifier and provenance limitations remain open.\n# D1 r9 Jupiter: incomplete preflight\n\nStatus: **incomplete; no bounded tagged run, corpus correspondence record, or verification result**. No declaration is finalized and no reliance is authorized. No commit was made.\n\n## Findings and refusal stage\n\n- Host `jupiter.stigmergy.systems`, Linux x86-64, OpenJDK 21.0.2, worktree base `747e80fce0428f6971299819b1623b99e01f4bb0`. The frozen site-table SHA-256 is `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`.\n- **Preflight / retained inputs:** the r8 findings explicitly say the measurement store was deleted. No `target/d1-*` store was present here. The r8 outcome entries retain measurements and admission origins, but the batch driver discards the input and snapshot addresses returned internally by the per-fixture persistence operation. Thus its outcomes do not provide the persisted input closure or a corpus record for the requested run. Recreating it requires fresh admissions and recording the addresses; the historical origins must not be fabricated or substituted.\n- **Preflight / bounds:** the draft's heap and lane quantities are undeclared. Design §6.3 also requires trace bytes and occupancy, row/occurrence/path bounds, phase time and peak memory, and baseline/added-work evidence. This session did not measure these quantities on Jupiter. The laptop ceilings and historical 4M/1M limits are not Jupiter measurements. Declaring the missing values from those figures would misrepresent their basis. No tagged lane was executed and there is no exceedance or passing verdict.\n- **Verification / publication:** `check-fixture!` explicitly does not establish admission authenticity, image derivations, trace completeness, durable retrieval or reliance. `attributed?` assumes already-scoped complete lane inputs; it does not validate them. The existing record and recipe functions are structural storage preconditions, not a complete seven-step verifier. The r7/r8 findings also identify the unpinned executable recipe/dependency and expansion closure and missing image/provenance derivation checks. A result record claiming verification, or a companion purporting to name the absent corpus record, would therefore be unsupported. Neither was published. Retention location and ownership remain the owner's pending decision; no 700 MiB store was committed.\n\n## Validation\n\nOn Jupiter, `test-d1-persisted-per-fixture-static-gate` passed 1 test / 4 assertions. This is one temporary-store static pilot, not a corpus or lane measurement. The standing `yang.correspondence-test`, `yang.correspondence-text-test`, `yang.correspondence-verifier-test`, `yang.correspondence-batch-test`, and `yang.clojure-callsite-test` battery passed 57 tests / 700 assertions, zero failures and errors. No code changed, so no new kondo run was made. The full oracle suite was not run.\n\n## Remaining work\n\nRebuild and retain the corpus under fresh captured coordinates while recording all addresses; complete and validate its recipe and record closure; implement red-first bounded measurement and trace-completeness checks, measure the static and lane quantities on this host, then publish a finite pre-run declaration. Only after that declaration may the independent verifier execute both lanes under the fixed ceilings. Publish the separate result and companion only when their referenced records and outcomes actually exist. Preserve any exceedance as a failed run.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
